{"kind":"request","text":"Replaces request aedb97c3, retired because its condition contradicted itself. Same defect, corrected condition.\n\nThe documentation map's ordered new-reader path goes \"Why gitseq exists\", then the end-to-end walkthrough, then concepts, then reference. It never names docs/getting-started.md, so a reader entering through the map is sent to a page of runnable commands without being told to build the binaries or create a workroom, both of which end-to-end assumes. The front README links getting-started; the map does not.\n\nWhat I now want, stated without the contradiction: getting-started belongs between \"Why\" and the end-to-end walkthrough. \"Why\" stays first because it is ten minutes with no commands, and a reader should know what the system is for before building anything.","body":{"conditions":"docs/README.md names docs/getting-started.md in its ordered new-reader path, positioned after \"Why gitseq exists\" and before the end-to-end walkthrough, with one clause saying what the step gives the reader — built binaries and a workroom — so the ordering reads as a reason rather than a list; the remaining entries keep their order relative to each other; the docset anchor gate passes, confirming the link resolves; `make test` passes; reviewed by codex at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}